Chapter 5.11 – Repository Structure and Project Organization
5.11.1 Overview

The MHMR Data Processing Framework is organized into multiple notebooks, Lakehouse schemas, and configuration objects within Microsoft Fabric. Each component is designed with a specific responsibility to ensure modularity, maintainability, and scalability.

The project follows a layered architecture where data flows from the source system through the Staging, History, and Snapshot layers while maintaining centralized logging and auditing.

5.11.2 Project Structure

Note: Replace the notebook names below with the exact names from your Fabric workspace if they differ.

MHMR Workspace

│
├── Lakehouse
│      ├── RAW
│      ├── PREV_RAW
│      ├── STAGING
│      ├── HISTORY
│      ├── SNAPSHOT
│      └── LOGS
│
├── Notebooks
│      ├── STAGING Notebook
│      ├── HISTORY Notebook
│      ├── SNAPSHOT Notebook
│
├── Configuration
│      ├── Table List
│      ├── Key Columns
│      ├── Constants
│      └── Global Variables
│
└── Monitoring
       ├── Daily Logs
       └── Final Logs
5.11.3 Lakehouse Organization

The project stores data in separate schemas to isolate each stage of the processing lifecycle.

Schema	Purpose
RAW	Stores the latest data received from source systems.
PREV_RAW	Stores the previous execution's source data for comparison.
STAGING	Stores records identified as Inserts, Updates, and Deletes.
HISTORY	Maintains historical versions of records using SCD Type 2.
SNAPSHOT	Maintains the latest version of each business record.
LOGS	Stores execution logs, audit details, and monitoring information.
5.11.4 Notebook Responsibilities
Staging Notebook

Purpose

Compare Raw and Previous Raw data.
Detect Inserts, Updates, and Deletes.
Generate row hashes.
Load staging tables.
Update Previous Raw.
Generate staging logs.

Output

Staging Tables
Daily Logs
Final Logs
Previous Raw Refresh
History Notebook

Purpose

Read Staging data.
Apply SCD Type 2 logic.
Maintain Active and Inactive records.
Support backfill processing.
Generate execution logs.

Output

History Tables
Daily Logs
Final Logs
Snapshot Notebook

Purpose

Read Staging data.
Build the latest business snapshot.
Replace outdated records.
Maintain one current version of each business key.
Generate execution logs.

Output

Snapshot Tables
Daily Logs
Final Logs
5.11.5 Configuration Components

The framework uses centralized configuration to minimize hard-coded values.

Configuration includes:

Table Names
Business Key Columns
Maximum Retry Count
Sleep Duration Between Retries
Maximum Parallel Workers
Timezone Settings
Catalog Names
Schema Names
Log Table Names

This allows new tables to be onboarded with minimal code changes.

5.11.6 Log Tables

Separate log tables are maintained for each framework.

Staging Logs

Capture

Processing Status
Table Name
Processing Date
Insert Count
Update Count
Delete Count
Start Time
End Time
Duration
History Logs

Capture

Processing Status
Insert Count
Update Count
Delete Count
Active Records
Retry Count
Error Details
Execution Duration
Snapshot Logs

Capture

Processing Status
Snapshot Records
Retry Count
Start Time
End Time
Error Details
5.11.7 Configuration Parameters
Parameter	Description
Catalog	MHMR_LAKEHOUSE
Session Timezone	America/Chicago
Retry Count	Configurable
Retry Delay	Configurable
Parallel Workers	Configurable
Auto Merge	Enabled
Delta Tables	Enabled
5.11.8 Execution Sequence

The notebooks are executed in the following order:

Raw Data Arrival
        │
        ▼
Staging Notebook
        │
        ▼
History Notebook
        │
        ▼
Snapshot Notebook
        │
        ▼
Audit Logs

Each notebook depends on the successful completion of the previous stage to ensure data consistency across the pipeline.

